  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "Is a triangle with side lengths \\(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{3},\\sqrt{5}\\) a right triangle?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "Yes, because \\(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{3}=\\sqrt{5}\\).",
    "B": "Yes, because \\((\\sqrt{2})^2+(\\sqrt{3})^2=(\\sqrt{5})^2\\), and the hypotenuse is \\(\\sqrt{5}\\).",
    "C": "No, because \\(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{3}\\ne\\sqrt{5}\\)."
  },
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "The longest side is \\(\\sqrt{5}\\). Since \\((\\sqrt{2})^2+(\\sqrt{3})^2=2+3=5=(\\sqrt{5})^2\\), the converse of the Pythagorean theorem applies. It is a right triangle with hypotenuse \\(\\sqrt{5}\\).",
